::meta{crate: tomet-shelf-document, dependencies: list(tomet-shelf-config, tomet-shelf-route, tomet-shelf-search), dependents: list(tomet-shelf-assets, tomet-shelf-headless, tomet-shelf-incremental, tomet-shelf-render)}

=[tomet-shelf-document]

Document parsing, AST transformation, and metadata extraction for `tomet-shelf`.

==[Module html]

Building blocks for the HTML this crate assembles by hand.

Infobox values reach the template through `| safe`, so anything produced
here has to be safe to drop straight into the page.

===[Functions]

====[fn escape_html]

Escape text for interpolation into HTML text nodes and double-quoted attributes.

====[fn sanitize_color_hex]

Normalize a `@meta.colors` entry to a bare hex body (`#AABBCC` -> `aabbcc`).

Returns `None` for anything that is not a 3/4/6/8-digit hex color, which keeps
unvetted text out of the inline `style` attributes that consume it.

====[fn color_chip_html]

A colour swatch, or plain escaped text when the value is not a hex colour.

====[fn external_link_html]

====[fn is_external_url]

==[Module icon]

===[Enums]

====[enum DocIcon]

Structured representation of a document icon.

Documents never produce HTML strings for icons. Instead, they produce
structured `DocIcon` variants, delegating presentation markup to `tomet-shelf-render`
or shelf UI engines (e.g. Leptos, Svelte, Web Components).

==[Module links]

Resolving the link references that appear inside `@meta` values.

The body's links are resolved upstream by `tomet-transform`; these are the
ones that only exist as metadata strings -- `parent: "[[unit]]"` and friends
-- which never pass through the renderer.

===[Structs]

====[struct ParsedLinkRef]

A parsed link reference representation.

===[Functions]

====[fn is_link_ref]

====[fn parse_link_ref]

Extract the core target and optional alias from link reference syntax.

====[fn clean_ref_target]

Clean link references like `@link(ref:"@foo")` or `ref:bar` into plain labels.

====[fn resolve_meta_link]

Resolve a `@meta` value that looks like a link into `(href, label)`.

Returns `None` when the value is not a link reference at all. An empty href
means the reference is a link to a page that does not exist yet.

====[fn format_meta_value_to_html]

Render a `@meta` value as infobox HTML: a link when it is one, escaped text otherwise.

====[fn resolve_meta_media_path]

Resolve a media reference in `@meta` (e.g. `@link(ref:+hash.png)`) to a URL.

====[fn outgoing_page_slugs]

The pages this document links to, as slugs, in the order they appear.

Collected from the parsed body before link resolution rewrites the targets,
then resolved the same way `@meta` references are. Only links that land on
another Tomet document count -- media and external URLs are not pages, and
a document linking to itself is not a backlink.

====[fn slug_from_href]

The slug a resolved `@meta` href points at, if it points at a page at all.

==[Module meta]

Turning a document's `@meta` block into the page's chrome: the hero chips,
the infobox rows, and the images the template hangs off them.

===[Module defaults]

====[Functions]

=====[fn hero_chips_ja]

Default hero chips in Japanese.

=====[fn hero_chips_en]

Default hero chips in English.

=====[fn default_hero_chips_for]

Look up default hero chips for a given book language code.

=====[fn key_labels_ja]

Default infobox key labels in Japanese.

=====[fn key_labels_en]

Default infobox key labels in English.

=====[fn default_key_labels_for]

Look up default infobox labels for a given book language code.

=====[fn resolve_label]

Resolve label for a given infobox key, falling back to default language labels and then the key itself.

===[Structs]

====[struct HeroChipItem]

====[struct InfoboxRowItem]

====[struct MetaProperties]

Everything the page template reads out of `@meta`.

===[Functions]

====[fn extract]

Read the page's chrome out of `@meta`.

`kind` is the document's element kind, used as an infobox row when the
metadata does not already say what this page is.

==[Module toc]

The table of contents and the sticky-tab tree built from it.

===[Structs]

====[struct TocItem]

====[struct TocNode]

===[Functions]

====[fn toc_from_outline]

Turn the renderer's heading outline into the page TOC, alongside the first
content H1 (used as a title fallback).

Only levels 1-4 make it into the TOC; deeper headings are structure the
sticky tabs have no room for.

====[fn build_section_tabs]

Fold a flat TOC into the tree the sticky-tab header renders.

Roots are the shallowest level present (H1 when the document has any),
and every deeper heading nests under the most recent shallower one.

===[Type Aliases]

====[type SectionTab]

==[Structs]

===[struct ProcessedDoc]

==[Functions]

===[fn extract_workspace_config_blocks]

Extract the `::config`/`::settings` blocks of a workspace-wide document.

===[fn inject_workspace_config_blocks]

Prepend pre-parsed workspace-wide config blocks so shared macros are in scope.

===[fn parse_tomet_document]

Read a document far enough to know what it is.

The build has to decide what the site publishes before it resolves a
single link, since a link to a document the site leaves out has to come
out broken. That decision needs the `::kind`, and the `::kind` needs a
parse -- so the parse is done once, here, and handed on.

===[fn process_tomet_document]

===[fn process_parsed_document]

===[fn process_parsed_document_with_blocks]

Turn a parsed document into the page it becomes using pre-parsed workspace config blocks.

===[fn process_parsed_document_with_custom_element]

Turn a parsed document into the page it becomes using pre-parsed workspace config blocks
and an optional custom element renderer (e.g. for `@doc.icon`).
